          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The north primarily denotes the direction from which hostile invasion and divine judgment arrive, historically associated with Mesopotamian powers; in a distinct recognized motif, it denotes God’s mountain or divine dwelling.\",\"book_summary\":\"The north is the hidden quarter from which concealed invaders emerge, especially the coalition that destroys Babylon; it is also the location of God’s mountain and the seat sought by the enemy.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Direction or source of hostile invasion and judgment; secondarily, God’s mountain or divine seat.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"A concealed northern quarter that produces invading forces, particularly Babylon’s spoilers, and also contains God’s mountain or desired divine seat.\",\"core_relation\":\"COMPATIBLE_OVERLAP\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book preserves both recognized consensus motifs—northern invasion and the divine mountain—but reclassifies the invasion motif through concealment and centers it on nations spoiling Babylon. It also extends that pattern toward Revelation 17’s destroyers of apocalyptic Babylon, although Revelation itself does not identify them as northern.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"No direct contradiction exists. The book does not replace the conventional invasion referent or deny the divine-mountain motif; it adds a hiddenness principle and a more specific Babylon-centered application not required by consensus interpretation.\",\"rationale\":\"Reduced to their minimum identifications, both readings associate north with invading forces and with God’s mountain. The book is therefore not divergent or wholly novel. It is refined because 'hidden quarter' and 'concealed invaders' make concealment intrinsic to the symbol, while the anti-Babylon and Revelation linkage materially reclassifies and expands selected cases beyond the ordinary geographical-prophetic account.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"A strong case for MATCH is that the entry’s two concrete claims—invaders coming from the north and God’s mountain being in the north—exactly reproduce recognized consensus motifs, while 'hidden' may be only descriptive wordplay. Nevertheless, the repeated concealment language and the Revelation 17 application appear central enough to constitute a material refinement rather than immaterial wording.\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"}"
        }
